\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces Visual estimators against the optical-tracker ground truth on the real \repo {dataset_realv3_000} recording. Planar ATE after rigid $SE(2)$ alignment with scale fixed at one, evaluated at the tracker's own samples ($n$); the time offset between the unsynchronised robot clock and the tracker is estimated from wheel-speed correlation (\cref {sec:method-trajectory}). Translational RPE at $\Delta =\SI {1}{\second }$ compares relative displacements in the aligned frame; the tracker has no heading, so rotational RPE is not defined. ``Path'' is the estimate's planar path length over the matched interval divided by the tracker's. The marker-to-sensor lever arm is not measured (horizontal offset assumed zero), so every value is provisional. Failed runs (\repo {dataset_realv3_000} VINS-Fusion) are excluded and reported in the text. Lower is better.\relax }}{77}{table.caption.132}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Offline YOLO performance against projected Gazebo object ground truth.}}{79}{table.caption.138}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces Object-class assignment conditional on successful IoU association, pooled across the four dynamic datasets.\relax }}{82}{table.caption.142}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.13}{\ignorespaces The two simulation testbeds of Experiment\nobreakspace {}3. Both use the live detector at replay time; the September campaign ran at half replay rate with three repeats per condition, the October runs at full rate with one.\relax }}{84}{table.caption.146}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.14}{\ignorespaces Relogged experiment progress and validity.}}{85}{table.caption.149}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.15}{\ignorespaces Anomaly catalogue for the relogged campaign.}}{85}{table.4.15}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.16}{\ignorespaces Extreme world, unfiltered arms: the best of the three September repeats per recording, ``best'' being the lowest aligned ATE. Rotation, jump-free RPE and jump count are those of the selected run; the median and worst ATE of the three are kept beside the best so the selection is visible. Jump-free RPE excludes pairs spanning a step above \SI {10}{\metre \per \second }; the rotational figure is the median over pairs, because a single orientation step inflates the RMSE (\cref {sec:realistic-ab}).\relax }}{88}{table.caption.154}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.17}{\ignorespaces Extreme world, filtered arms: every run outside the September repeat set, one row each (October 2026, replay rate 1.0, live detector). ``Match'' is \repo {detection_match_rate}; a run below 0.98 did not filter on every frame. ``Kept'' is the processed fraction of received frames. Loop-closer state is read from the log. ``Overlap'' is the span over which estimate and ground truth coexist, against the recording's length; a run covering less than \SI {80}{\percent } of it is a partial run and its ATE describes only that span. These runs are reported on their own, not as pairs: their baselines are the September repeats of \cref {tab:extreme-accuracy-best}, a different session and node build.\relax }}{88}{table.caption.158}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.18}{\ignorespaces Extreme world, unfiltered runs: onset of sustained error and its place on the route, per run, under the same definitions as \cref {tab:extreme-timelines-filtered}. One entry per run, in repeat order; ``never'' means the error stayed below the threshold for the whole run.\relax }}{93}{table.caption.161}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.19}{\ignorespaces Extreme world, filtered runs: when the error rises, where, and how long the filter ran. ``Onset'' is the first time the start-anchored position error stays above $3\times $ the estimator's static median for \SI {1}{\second } (threshold in the text); ``where'' gives the route progress at that moment and whether the robot was on a straight or in a heading change (ground-truth yaw rate above \SI {10}{\degree \per \second }). ``Filter until'' is the end of the initial stretch of \SI {5}{\second } windows with a mask on at least \SI {98}{\percent } of frames; ``unmasked'' is the image area left after masking on matched frames, median (minimum). Times are seconds from the ground-truth start.\relax }}{93}{table.caption.162}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.20}{\ignorespaces Extreme world, real-time and resource measurements: mean $\pm $ SD over the three September repeats per recording ($n$), with a pooled row per scene. Front-end and back-end are each run's median module latency (ORB-SLAM3: tracking and local mapping; VINS-Fusion: feature tracking and back-end optimisation); throughput is processed frames per second of sensor time; ``Kept'' the processed fraction of received frames; CPU is process time over wall time, in percent of one core; RSS the peak resident set. Replay rate $0.5\times $. The VINS-Fusion+YOLO rows are the September runs, in which the filter did not activate except on \repo {dynamic_00_000} repeat\nobreakspace {}1 (re-run 15 October).\relax }}{101}{table.caption.171}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.21}{\ignorespaces Unfiltered arms on the four realistic-warehouse recordings, 21 October 2026, replay rate 1.0. Aligned ATE is RMSE after a rigid $SE(3)$ fit with scale fixed; RPE is translational, at $\Delta =\SI {1}{\second }$ over pairs that do not span a jump; ``Jumps'' counts steps above \SI {10}{\metre \per \second }; ``Kept'' is the fraction of the bag's stereo pairs the estimator processed (frames lost before the node's own queue count as lost). Loop-closer state is read from \repo {loop_closing.csv}, not from the run name. Static and dynamic rows of one layout share a world geometry and differ in traffic only.\relax }}{103}{table.caption.175}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.22}{\ignorespaces Every filtered run on the dynamic realistic recordings, with the three admissibility gates of \cref {sec:vio-filter-method}. ``Match'' is \repo {detection_match_rate}, the fraction of frames that carried a current detector mask; below 0.98 the filter did not run. ``Input'' is the processed-frame count relative to the paired baseline. ``Binary'' says whether the run and its baseline share the node build of 16 October 2026. A run passes only when all three hold; ATE and RPE are listed for every run but may be read as a treatment effect only on a passing row. Runs that loaded a prior atlas are outside the A/B and carry no baseline.\relax }}{105}{table.caption.179}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.23}{\ignorespaces Realistic world, real-time and resource measurements, one row per run (replay rate 1.0, live detector). Columns as in \cref {tab:extreme-resources}; ``Kept'' here is the processed fraction of the bag's frames, so frames lost before the node's own queue count as lost. Accuracy for the same runs is in \cref {tab:realistic-baseline,tab:realistic-filtered}.\relax }}{106}{table.caption.182}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.24}{\ignorespaces Realistic world, unfiltered runs: onset of sustained error and its place on the route, per run, under the same definitions as \cref {tab:realistic-timelines-filtered}. One entry per run, in repeat order; ``never'' means the error stayed below the threshold for the whole run.\relax }}{107}{table.caption.184}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.25}{\ignorespaces Realistic world, filtered runs: when the error rises, where, and how long the filter ran. ``Onset'' is the first time the start-anchored position error stays above $3\times $ the estimator's static median for \SI {1}{\second } (threshold in the text); ``where'' gives the route progress at that moment and whether the robot was on a straight or in a heading change (ground-truth yaw rate above \SI {10}{\degree \per \second }). ``Filter until'' is the end of the initial stretch of \SI {5}{\second } windows with a mask on at least \SI {98}{\percent } of frames; ``unmasked'' is the image area left after masking on matched frames, median (minimum). Times are seconds from the ground-truth start.\relax }}{107}{table.caption.185}%
